<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhengguo Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">246 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Zheng Guo Canal, Zhengguo Qu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zhengguo Canal was an irrigation canal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>, begun in 246 BCE and named after its designer, Zheng Guo, an engineer from the State of Han. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, Han sent him to exhaust <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>'s resources with a huge project, but the canal, linking the Jing and Luo rivers, irrigated the plain north of the Wei and greatly increased Qin's harvests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhengguo-canal.jpg" alt="Zhengguo Canal" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Weirivermap.png: Kmusser derivative work: Felis domestica (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhengguo_Canal_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
